      "id": "experience-system_bottlenecks",
      "kind": "experience",
      "text": "Lex Fridman asked: Why is designing at rack scale hard? Splitting the workload across machines brings communication and coordination costs into play. He invokes Amdahl's law to explain why improving compute alone has limited effect. Patrick Collison asked: Would NVIDIA have had the same AI success without CUDA? He describes CUDA as essential and explains the economics of accelerating a small number of routines that consume much of a program's runtime.",
      "provenance": "source_summary",
      "evidence_ids": [
        "jensen-lex-494-01",
        "jensen-stripe-sessions-2024-04"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "system_bottlenecks",
      "kind": "value",
      "text": "Evaluate the whole system and prioritize its limiting part; a faster component alone may not improve the result users experience.",
      "provenance": "editorial_interpretation",
